# .trivyignore — checked-in allowlist for Trivy image scans.
#
# Policy: every entry MUST be preceded by a comment that explains the
# justification (why the finding is acceptable here). Bare entries are
# rejected at review time. Prefer fixing over allowlisting whenever a
# patched package is available.
#
# Format: one Trivy ID per line (typically CVE-YYYY-NNNN, but Trivy may
# also emit GHSA-* / DLA-* / DSA-* IDs). Lines starting with '#' are
# comments. Blank lines are allowed.
#
# Scope of the gate (set in .github/workflows/ci.yml):
#   severity:        HIGH,CRITICAL
#   ignore-unfixed:  true     # finding is hidden if no patched version exists
#   gate:            hard-fail (exit-code: 1) — set by CI-8.

# ===========================================================================
# Block 1 — Debian-9 vendor-lock CVEs in tidal-connect image (CI-6 baseline).
#
# Source of the lock: tidal-connect/Dockerfile lines 28–35 deliberately
# downgrade libssl1.0.0 and libcurl3 to **Debian-8** builds
# (1.0.1t-1+deb8u12 / 7.38.0-4+deb8u16) pulled from snapshot.debian.org,
# because tidal_connect_application requires those exact symbol versions.
# See AGENTS.md §2 "library pins are intentional" for the full SONAME
# inventory verified by ldd in CI.
#
# Why none of these are fixable for us:
#   - Trivy's suggested "Fixed Version" column points at Debian-9 OpenSSL
#     1.1.0 backports (e.g. 1.1.0l-1~deb9u5). The vendor binary loads
#     libssl.so.1.0.0 / libcrypto.so.1.0.0 (OpenSSL 1.0.x SONAMEs only)
#     — bumping to 1.1.x breaks ldd resolution and the binary fails to
#     start. The TIDAL Connect protocol has no FOSS replacement
#     (AGENTS.md §2) so re-linking is not an option.
#   - libldap-2.4-2 is pulled transitively by libcurl3; Debian-9 ELTS
#     ships 2.4.44+dfsg-5+deb9u9 only via the paid Freexian channel,
#     which is not on snapshot.debian.org.
#
# Compensating controls: container runs read-only paths where possible,
# `network_mode: host` is on a trusted LAN only (consumer Snapcast
# deployment), and exposure to remote curl/openssl input is limited to
# the TIDAL backend (HTTPS to api.tidal.com, controlled by the binary).
#
# Revisit conditions: any of (a) successful base-image swap by the
# base-image-modernizer agent (BASE-1/2), (b) iFi/vendor publishing a
# new binary with newer SONAMEs, (c) ELTS subscription added.
#
# CVE list extracted from CI run 28457038986 (commit d241204). Re-verify
# the set whenever Trivy DB updates surface new fixable HIGH/CRITICAL
# findings in libcurl3 / libldap-2.4-2 / libssl1.0.0.
# ===========================================================================

